{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And he saw that rest was good.</b> He rejected the way of war and armies and saw that the rest of Torah was good. This is the reasoning of Torah: that it requires rest of the body and exertion of the soul, the reverse of worldly pursuits, which require exertion of the body and rest of the soul. And because the pursuit of Torah requires leisure and bodily tranquility, and it is impossible for it to be maintained in one who has distractions and sighs, therefore our Rabbis of blessed memory enacted for us in the prayer: 'Restore our judges as at first, and our counselors as in the beginning, and remove from us sorrow and sighing.' And the intention of the one who prays is not that the Holy One, blessed be He, restore to us the judges and counselors in order to press him with his obligatory affairs — nor did Isaiah intend this when he said (Isaiah 1:26): ״ואשיבה שופטיך כבראשונה ויועציך כבתחלה״. Rather, the essence of that blessing and the word of the prophet is the secret of the transition (עבור): we ask the Holy One, blessed be He, to restore to us the judges who would sit in the Chamber of Hewn Stone to judge among us as at first, and to give counsel as in the beginning, and then all Israel would be prepared to receive the intelligibles and to attain at that time knowledge of HaShem, blessed be He, in the manner of what is written (Isaiah 11:9): ״כי מלאה הארץ דעה את ה'״, and therefore it is joined to this: 'and remove from us sorrow and sighing' — for it is impossible to attain knowledge of HaShem to perfection with sorrow and sighing, but only with joy and with rest. And behold, they enacted this blessing after the blessing of תקע בשופר, which speaks of the ingathering of the exiles." "<b>That it was good.</b> As in (Proverbs 4:2): ״כי לקח טוב נתתי לכם״. <b>And the land,</b> from the root of (Ezekiel 36:20): ״ומארצו יצאו״, which is translated ״ומאוריתיה״. <b>That it was pleasant,</b> after (Proverbs 3:17): ״דרכיה דרכי נועם״. <b>And he bowed his shoulder to bear,</b> the yoke of Torah, and likewise it is written at the giving of Torah (Psalms 18:10): ״ויט שמים וירד״. And ויט שכמו לסבול includes further that he humbled himself, and from here it is fitting for all learners of Torah to have within them the trait of humility. <b>And he became a servant under tribute.</b> That he was subservient to all Israel and produced for them tribute in rulings and responses and calculations of the intercalation, in the manner of what is written in Chronicles (1 Chronicles 12): ״ומבני יששכר יודעי בינה לעתים לדעת מה יעשה ישראל ראשיהם מאתים וכל אחיהם על פיהם״." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> 'Issachar is a strong-boned ass' — 'a donkey caused it.' When Jacob came from the field in the evening, Leah heard the voice of the donkey as it brayed; immediately ״ותצא לאה לקראתו ותאמר אלי תבוא״, and it is written (Genesis 30:16): ״וישכב עמה בלילה הוא״. The verse reads: 'Issachar is a strong-boned ass' — 'a donkey caused it' (חמור גרם): a donkey caused it to him." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah:</b> 'Issachar is a strong-boned ass' — he mentions in him 'lying down' (רובץ), and so it says (Deuteronomy 22): ״והאם רובצת״, and this is the expression ״בין המשפתים״ — 'between the borders,' ״בין תבין״ — 'you will understand,' for thus it is written: ״ומבני יששכר יודעי בינה״ — there is the house of the soul, its rest and its inheritance, and therefore it is adjacent to it: ״וירא מנוחה״, and the translation of Onkelos: ״ואחסנתיה בין תחומיא״, and this is clear."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:21:43.673905898Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}